To show the search control on your leaflet map, add the SearchControl to the leaflet map

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  style: 'bar',
});

map.addControl(searchControl);

Style

<Map provider="OpenStreetMap" controlOptions={{ style: 'button' }} />

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  style: 'button',
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
});

map.addControl(searchControl);

style: 'bar' shows the search box as a bar at the top of the map, in the middle. position only places the button style, the bar stays at the top.

Message if address not found

<Map
provider="OpenStreetMap"
controlOptions={{
notFoundMessage: 'Sorry, that address could not be found.',
}}
/>

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  notFoundMessage: 'Sorry, that address could not be found.',
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  style: 'bar',
});

map.addControl(searchControl);

Multiple providers

With providers instead of provider, a select left of the input lets the user pick the provider to search with, or search all of them at once. In that case every suggestion shows the name of its provider. Picking another provider searches the typed text again.

<Map providers={['OpenStreetMap', 'Photon', 'Esri']} />

import {
  SearchControl,
  OpenStreetMapProvider,
  PhotonProvider,
  EsriProvider,
} from 'leaflet-geosearch';

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  providers: [
    { label: 'OpenStreetMap', provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider() },
    { label: 'Photon', provider: new PhotonProvider() },
    { label: 'Esri', provider: new EsriProvider() },
  ],
  allProvidersLabel: 'All', // false leaves the "all" option out
  style: 'bar',
});

map.addControl(searchControl);

The labels are only shown to the user, so they can be in any language. When one provider fails while searching all of them, the others still show their results.

How many suggestions

maxSuggestions (default 5) is the size of the whole list, not the number per provider. When all providers are searched, they take turns: the first result of each provider, then the second of each, and so on, until the list is full. With the three providers above and the default of 5, that's 2 from OpenStreetMap, 2 from Photon and 1 from Esri.

For the same number from each provider, set maxSuggestions to a multiple of the number of providers:

<Map
providers={['OpenStreetMap', 'Photon', 'Esri']}
controlOptions={{ maxSuggestions: 9 }}
/>

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  providers: [
    { label: 'OpenStreetMap', provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider() },
    { label: 'Photon', provider: new PhotonProvider() },
    { label: 'Esri', provider: new EsriProvider() },
  ],
  maxSuggestions: 9, // up to 3 from each provider
});

A provider with fewer results leaves its turns to the others. With a single provider selected, the list shows up to maxSuggestions of its results.

Falling back to another provider

fallback searches the next provider in the list when the selected one fails ('error'), or also when it finds nothing ('empty'). With the select hidden, the user sees a single search box that keeps working when the first provider is down:

<Map
providers={['OpenStreetMap', 'Photon']}
controlOptions={{
showProviderSelect: false,
allProvidersLabel: false,
fallback: 'error',
}}
/>

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  providers: [
    { label: 'OpenStreetMap', provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider() },
    { label: 'Photon', provider: new PhotonProvider() },
  ],
  showProviderSelect: false,
  allProvidersLabel: false,
  fallback: 'error',
});

From code

searchControl.selectProvider(1); // the index in providers, or 'all'
searchControl.getSelectedProvider(); // { index: 1, label: 'Photon', provider }
searchControl.getProviders(); // [{ index: 0, label: 'OpenStreetMap', provider }, ...]
searchControl.setProviderSelectVisible(false);

map.on('geosearch/providerchange', ({ provider }) => {
  console.log(`searching with ${provider.label}`);
});

resultFormat, popupFormat and the events also get the provider that found a result, e.g. resultFormat: ({ result, provider }) => ....

Zoom on a result

Results with bounds (most OpenStreetMap results) are fitted into the map, results without bounds are shown at zoomLevel. zoomToBounds: false shows every result at zoomLevel, fitBoundsOptions goes to Leaflet's fitBounds.

<Map
provider="OpenStreetMap"
controlOptions={{ zoomToBounds: false, zoomLevel: 16 }}
/>

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  zoomToBounds: false,
  zoomLevel: 16,
  // or keep fitting the bounds, but not too close and away from the edges
  // fitBoundsOptions: { maxZoom: 16, padding: [50, 50] },
});

flyTo: true flies to the result with Leaflet's flyTo and flyToBounds instead of jumping there. An object is passed to them, so { duration: 1.5 } sets how long the flight takes. The zoom options above still apply, and animateZoom: false turns the animation off for both.

<Map provider="OpenStreetMap" controlOptions={{ flyTo: { duration: 1.5 } }} />

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  flyTo: { duration: 1.5 },
});

Address of a dragged marker

With a draggable marker and reverseOnDragEnd: true, dropping the marker looks up the address at its new position. The provider that found the result is asked, so it has to support reverse geocoding. The popup shows the new address, and with keepResult the input does too. Drag the marker after a search:

<Map
provider="OpenStreetMap"
controlOptions={{
marker: { draggable: true },
reverseOnDragEnd: true,
keepResult: true,
showPopup: true,
}}
/>

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  marker: { draggable: true },
  reverseOnDragEnd: true,
  keepResult: true,
});

map.on('geosearch/marker/reverse', ({ location }) => {
  console.log(location.label);
});

Result list position

With the control in a bottom corner of the map, the result list would open below the input, outside the map. resultListPosition: 'above' opens it above the input, 'auto' does so when position is bottomleft or bottomright, for the button style. The bar is always at the top, so it keeps the list below. The default is 'below'.

<Map
provider="OpenStreetMap"
controlOptions={{
style: 'button',
position: 'bottomleft',
resultListPosition: 'auto',
}}
/>

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  style: 'button',
  position: 'bottomleft',
  resultListPosition: 'auto',
});

Popup

The marker of a result gets a popup with its label. popupOptions goes to Leaflet's bindPopup, popupFormat sets the content and showPopup opens it right away:

<Map
provider="OpenStreetMap"
controlOptions={{
showPopup: true,
popupOptions: { maxWidth: 180, className: 'my-popup' },
}}
/>

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  showPopup: true,
  popupOptions: { maxWidth: 180, className: 'my-popup' },
  popupFormat: ({ result }) => result.label,
});

markerPopup: false leaves the popup out, for example for a draggable marker that you handle yourself:

<Map
provider="OpenStreetMap"
controlOptions={{ markerPopup: false, marker: { draggable: true } }}
/>

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  markerPopup: false,
  marker: { draggable: true },
});

Theme

theme: 'dark' gives the control dark colors, 'auto' follows the color scheme of the system. The default 'light' and 'dark' stay the same whatever the system uses.

<Map provider="OpenStreetMap" controlOptions={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  theme: 'dark', // 'light' (default), 'dark' or 'auto'
});

The colors are css variables, so you can set your own:

.leaflet-control-geosearch {
  --geosearch-background: #fffaf0;
  --geosearch-text: #3b2f2f;
  --geosearch-hover: #f3e9d8;
  --geosearch-border: #d6c7ad;
  --geosearch-icon: #8a5a2b;
}

The variables are --geosearch-background, --geosearch-text, --geosearch-strong (the reset button), --geosearch-muted (the provider names), --geosearch-border, --geosearch-divider, --geosearch-hover, --geosearch-button-hover and --geosearch-icon.

Icons

searchIcon replaces the magnifying glass of the search button with your own html, like an svg. With currentColor it follows the theme. It is put into the page as html, so only pass markup you wrote yourself. resetButton is the text of the button that clears the search (× by default), and is always set as text.

<Map
provider="OpenStreetMap"
controlOptions={{
style: 'button',
resetButton: '✕',
searchIcon:
'',
}}
/>

const searchControl = new SearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  style: 'button',
  searchIcon:
    '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor">…</svg>',
  resetButton: '✕', // text of the button that clears the search
});

Accessibility

The control follows the combobox pattern of the ARIA authoring practices. The input is a combobox that controls the result list, the suggestions are options, and the arrow keys move the selection screen readers announce. The search and reset buttons are named by searchLabel and clearSearchLabel, which setLabels can change later.

Security

The labels come from the provider, and for OpenStreetMap they are edited by anyone. The default resultFormat and popupFormat escape them, and provider names, searchLabel, clearSearchLabel and setQuery are always set as text.

Some options are put into the page as html, on purpose:

Option	Put in as
resultFormat, popupFormat	html: escape the parts that come from the result
notFoundMessage (also through setLabels)	html
searchIcon	html

Only pass markup you wrote yourself to these, never text from users or from a provider without escaping it:

const escape = (text) =>
  String(text).replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) => `&#${c.charCodeAt(0)};`);

new SearchControl({
  provider,
  resultFormat: ({ result }) => `<b>${escape(result.raw.name)}</b>`,
});

API keys used in the browser can always be seen. Restrict them to your domain in the console of the service. With a Content Security Policy, allow the hosts of your provider in connect-src, and for the Bing provider, which answers through JSONP, dev.virtualearth.net in script-src. The Google provider loads the Maps JavaScript API from maps.googleapis.com.

Methods

Method	What it does
open(), close()	Opens or closes the search box (button style). close also clears it unless keepResult is on.
reset()	Clears the input, the suggestions and the markers, and drops a running search.
setQuery(text)	Puts text into the input without searching.
setLabels(labels)	Changes the texts: searchLabel, clearSearchLabel, notFoundMessage and providerSelectLabel.
selectProvider(index), getSelectedProvider(), getProviders(), setProviderSelectVisible(visible)	See Multiple providers.

For example, a map in a dialog that should start empty each time, with texts from your translations:

dialog.addEventListener('open', () => {
  searchControl.reset();
});

i18n.on('languageChanged', () => {
  searchControl.setLabels({
    searchLabel: i18n.t('search.placeholder'),
    clearSearchLabel: i18n.t('search.clear'),
    notFoundMessage: i18n.t('search.notFound'),
  });
});

Options

Option	Type	Default	Description
provider or providers 	Provider or { label, provider }[]		One of the two: the provider to search with, or several to choose from (see above).
showProviderSelect	boolean	true	Show the provider select (with more than one provider).
allProvidersLabel	string | false	'All'	The select option that searches all providers. false leaves it out.
providerSelectLabel	string	'Search with'	Accessible name of the provider select.
fallback	false | 'error' | 'empty'	false	Search the next provider when the selected one fails, or also when it finds nothing.
style	'button' | 'bar'	'button'	A button that opens the search box, or a bar that is always visible.
position	ControlPosition	'topleft'	Where Leaflet puts the control (button style).
autoComplete	boolean	true	Search while typing and show suggestions.
autoCompleteDelay	number	250	Milliseconds to wait after the last keystroke before searching.
minQueryLength	number	1	Characters to type before autocomplete searches. Pressing enter always searches.
maxSuggestions	number	5	How many suggestions to show.
cacheSize	number	0	How many search results to keep in memory, so the same text isn't requested twice. 0 turns the cache off.
showResultListOnSubmit	boolean	false	Enter shows the results to pick from instead of the first one. With autoComplete: false the provider is only searched on enter.
requeryOnSelect	boolean	false	Search the provider again when a suggestion is picked, even if it has coordinates.
showMarker	boolean	true	Show a marker at the result.
showPopup	boolean	false	Open a popup on the marker.
marker	MarkerOptions	{ draggable: false }	Options for the marker. Missing keys keep their default. Without icon, Leaflet's default icon is used.
maxMarkers	number	1	How many result markers stay on the map.
popupFormat	({ query, result, provider }) => string	the escaped label	The popup content (html).
resultFormat	({ result, provider }) => string	the escaped label	The text of a suggestion (html).
retainZoomLevel	boolean	false	Only pan to the result, keep the zoom level.
zoomLevel	number	18	Zoom level for results without bounds, or for every result with zoomToBounds: false.
zoomToBounds	boolean	true	Fit the map to the bounds of a result. false shows every result at zoomLevel.
resultListPosition	'below' | 'above' | 'auto'	'below'	Where the result list opens, see above.
theme	'light' | 'dark' | 'auto'	'light'	The colors of the control.
searchIcon	string	''	Html of the search button instead of the magnifying glass.
markerPopup	boolean	true	Give the marker a popup.
popupOptions	PopupOptions	{}	Passed to Leaflet's bindPopup.
reverseOnDragEnd	boolean	false	Look up the address when a draggable marker is dropped, see above.
fitBoundsOptions	FitBoundsOptions	{}	Passed to Leaflet's fitBounds, e.g. { maxZoom: 16, padding: [50, 50] }.
animateZoom	boolean	true	Animate moving to the result.
flyTo	boolean | ZoomPanOptions	false	Fly to the result instead of jumping there, see above.
updateMap	boolean	true	Move the map to the result.
autoClose	boolean	false	Close the control after a result is picked. The marker stays.
keepResult	boolean	false	Keep the picked result in the search box. Without it, closing the control also removes the marker.
searchLabel	string	'Enter address'	Placeholder of the search box.
clearSearchLabel	string	'Clear search'	Label of the reset button.
notFoundMessage	string	''	Shown when a search finds nothing.
messageHideDelay	number	3000	Milliseconds before the message hides.
resetButton	string	'×'	Text of the reset button.
classNames	object	see source	Extra class names per element. Missing keys keep their default.

required

Events

Event	Payload	When
geosearch/beforeshowlocation	{ location, query, provider, preventDefault }	A result is about to be shown.
geosearch/showlocation	{ location, marker, provider }	A result is shown on the map.
geosearch/marker/dragend	{ location, event }	A draggable result marker was moved.
geosearch/marker/reverse	{ location, marker, provider }	The address of a dropped marker was found (reverseOnDragEnd).
geosearch/error	{ error, query, source, provider }	A search failed. source is 'autocomplete' or 'submit'.
geosearch/providerchange	{ provider, providers }	Another provider was picked, by the user or selectProvider.

geosearch/beforeshowlocation

Fires before the marker is added and the map moves. event.preventDefault() skips both and geosearch/showlocation, so you can show the result your own way:

map.on('geosearch/beforeshowlocation', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  map.flyTo([event.location.y, event.location.x], 14);
});

geosearch/showlocation

location is the result as the provider returned it, marker the Leaflet marker on the map, and provider the provider that found the result. With a single provider, its label is ''.

map.on('geosearch/showlocation', ({ location, marker, provider }) => {
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ location, provider: provider.label }, null, 2));
});

{
  "location": {
    "x": 4.2990429,
    "y": 52.0990969,
    "label": "Madurodam, 1, George Maduroplein, Archipelbuurt, The Hague, South Holland, Netherlands, 2584RZ, The Netherlands",
    "bounds": [
      [52.0990469, 4.2989929],
      [52.0991469, 4.2990929]
    ],
    "raw": {
      "place_id": 275030941,
      "osm_type": "node",
      "osm_id": 2590910713,
      "class": "tourism",
      "type": "theme_park"
    }
  },
  "provider": "OpenStreetMap"
}

x is the longitude and y the latitude. raw is the full answer of the provider, shortened here.

geosearch/marker/dragend

Only with marker: { draggable: true }. location is the new position of the marker, event the Leaflet dragend event.

map.on('geosearch/marker/dragend', ({ location, event }) => {
  console.log(JSON.stringify(location, null, 2));
});

{
  "lat": 52.0993,
  "lng": 4.3001
}

geosearch/marker/reverse

Only with reverseOnDragEnd: true. location is the place at the new position, marker the dropped marker and provider the provider that was asked.

map.on('geosearch/marker/reverse', ({ location, provider }) => {
  const { label, x, y } = location;
  console.log(
    JSON.stringify({ label, x, y, provider: provider.label }, null, 2),
  );
});

{
  "label": "Dam, Centrum, Amsterdam, Noord-Holland, Nederland, 1012 NP, Nederland",
  "x": 4.8926932,
  "y": 52.3733144,
  "provider": "OpenStreetMap"
}

geosearch/error

error is what the provider threw, query the searched text, source 'autocomplete', 'submit' or 'reverse', and provider the provider that failed. For 'reverse', query is the position as "lat,lng".

map.on('geosearch/error', ({ error, query, source, provider }) => {
  console.log(
    JSON.stringify(
      {
        message: error.message,
        query,
        source,
        provider: provider.label,
      },
      null,
      2,
    ),
  );
});

{
  "message": "Request failed with status 418",
  "query": "den haag",
  "source": "autocomplete",
  "provider": "OpenStreetMap"
}

geosearch/providerchange

Fired when another provider is picked in the select, or with selectProvider. provider is the same as getSelectedProvider() returns, providers the same as getProviders(): every provider of the control with its index and label.

map.on('geosearch/providerchange', ({ provider, providers }) => {
  const short = ({ index, label }) => ({ index, label });
  console.log(
    JSON.stringify(
      { provider: short(provider), providers: providers.map(short) },
      null,
      2,
    ),
  );
});

{
  "provider": {
    "index": 1,
    "label": "Photon"
  },
  "providers": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "label": "OpenStreetMap"
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "label": "Photon"
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "label": "Esri"
    }
  ]
}

For the "All" option provider is { "index": "all", "label": "All" }. Each entry of providers also has the provider instance itself, left out above.

note: AMap services require an API key. Obtain here.
For more options and configurations, see the AMap developer docs.

const provider = new AMapProvider({
  params: {
    key: '__YOUR_AMAP_KEY__',
  },
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

note: Bing services require an API key. Obtain here.
For more options and configurations, see the Microsoft developer docs.

const provider = new BingProvider({
  params: {
    key: '__YOUR_BING_KEY__',
  },
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

It's possible to create your own providers either by creating one from scratch, or extending an existing one.

The most trivial one to extend, is the OpenStreetMap provider. As Nominatim is open source, and can be self hosted, the provider allows for easy extension.

class MyProvider extends OpenStreetMapProvider {
  constructor(options) {
    super({
      ...options,
      searchUrl: 'https://example.com/api/search',
      reverseUrl: 'https://example.com/api/reverse',
    });
  }
}

When the constructor is the only thing you need to override, it might be sufficient to provide the options during initialization:

const provider = new OpenStreetMapProvider({
  searchUrl: 'https://example.com/api/search',
  reverseUrl: 'https://example.com/api/reverse',
});

However, custom providers can be easier to share, and have a single source of truth. As you don't need to manage the URL's on various places throughout your codebase.

Custom Server Response

When you need to adjust the provider to support a different json data shape, you might want to override more than just the urls.

Theparse method allows you to transform the server response, to the SearchResult types.

The endpoint method is there to select the correct endpoint, based on the search parameters.

class MyProvider extends JsonProvider {
  endpoint({ query, type }) {
    // Result: https://example.com/api/search?q=some%20address&f=json
    return this.getUrl('https://example.com/api/search', {
      q: query,
      f: 'json',
    });
  }

  parse({ data }) {
    // Note that `data` is the raw result returned by the server. This
    // method should return data in the SearchResult format.
    return data.map((x) => ({
      x: data.x,
      y: data.y,
      label: data.label,
      bounds: data.bounds,
    }));
  }
}

Sharing

When you've build a custom provider that can be used by others, please consider opening a pull-request, or publishing it to npm.

For more options and configurations, see the ArcGIS developer docs.

const provider = new EsriProvider();

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

The provider asks for up to 5 locations. Pass maxLocations in params to change that:

const provider = new EsriProvider({ params: { maxLocations: 10 } });

API key

Without a key the provider uses the free geocode.arcgis.com service. With an ArcGIS API key, pass it as token. ArcGIS asks for authenticated requests to go to geocode-api.arcgis.com, so the provider sends them there:

const provider = new EsriProvider({ params: { token: '__YOUR_ARCGIS_KEY__' } });

For more options and configurations, see the Geo Api FR.

const provider = new GeoApiFrProvider();

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Optional parameters

Geo Api FR supports a number of optional parameters. As the api requires those parameters to be added to the url, they can be added to the params key of the provider.

All options defined next to the params key, would have been added to the request body.

const provider = new GeoApiFrProvider({
  searchUrl: 'https://data.geopf.fr/geocodage/search',
  reverseUrl: 'https://data.geopf.fr/geocodage/reverse',
  params: {
    type: 'municipality', // limit search results to city
    autocomplete: 1, // Use in autocomplete mode (search in prefix mode)
    lat: 0, // Latitude in degree
    lon: 0, // Longitude in degree
  },
});

note: Geoapify services require an API key. Obtain geoapify.
For more options and configurations, see the Geoapify developer docs.

const provider = new GeoapifyProvider({
  params: {
    apiKey: '__YOUR_HERE_KEY__',
  },
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Geocode Earth is a hosted version of Pelias run by the core maintainers of the FOSS project.

note: Geocode Earth services require an API key, grab a free trial key from their website.

For more options and configurations, see the documentation.

// grab an API key from https://geocode.earth
const provider = new GeocodeEarthProvider({
  params: {
    api_key: '__YOUR_GEOCODE_EARTH_KEY__',
  },
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Optional parameters

Geocode Earth supports a wide range of number of optional parameters which can be applied to every request using the params object:

const provider = new GeocodeEarthProvider({
  params: {
    size: 5, // limit the total number of results returned
    lang: 'nl', // render results in Dutch
    'boundary.country': 'NL', // limit search results to the Netherlands
    layers: 'address,street', // limmit which layers are queried
  },
});

Or individually on a per-request basis:

const results = await provider.search({
  query: {
    text: 'example',
    'focus.point.lat': 1.11, // score results nearer to the focus point higher
    'focus.point.lon': 2.22,
  },
});

note: Google services require an API key enabled for both the Geocoding API and
the Maps JavaScript API (more on key configuration below). Obtain here. For more options and configurations,
see the Google Maps developer docs.

const provider = new GoogleProvider({ apiKey: '__YOUR_GOOGLE_KEY__' });

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Optional parameters

Google supports a number of optional parameters which must be specified when loading the JavaScript API.

const params = {
  apiKey: '__YOUR_GOOGLE_KEY__',
  language: 'nl', // render results in Dutch
  region: 'nl', // prioritize matches within The Netherlands
};

new GoogleProvider({ ...params });

The provider loads the Maps JavaScript API with @googlemaps/js-api-loader. These options are passed on: apiKey, version, language, region, libraries, mapIds, authReferrerPolicy, channel, client and solutionChannel. url, nonce, retries and id worked with older versions of the loader and are ignored now (with a warning in the console).

If the page already loaded the Maps JavaScript API (with a script tag, or another library like GoogleMutant), the provider uses it and doesn't load it again. The options above don't apply then.

Configuring your API key

Application restrictions (highly recommended)

Because your API key will be exposed with each request from the client, it is highly recommended that you should add an application restriction to any of your public-facing keys to limit the HTTP referrers from which requests will be accepted - for more information on how to configure this restriction, see the documentation.

API restrictions (optional)

Your may choose not to apply any API restrictions to your key, leaving it in an "Unrestricted" state. However, if you do wish to limit the APIs that can be accessed with your key, you will need to make sure that it as configured to at least make use of both the Geocoding API and the Maps JavaScript API in order for web client-based geocoding to work.

note: Here services require an API key. Obtain here.
For more options and configurations, see the Here developer docs.

const provider = new HereProvider({
  params: {
    apiKey: '__YOUR_HERE_KEY__',
  },
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

WARNING: This provider is unsafe to use and should be considered DEPRECATED.
It is strongly suggested to use the new Google Provider. If you're currently migrating from a previous version
and you still wish to use this provider you should pull in the LegacyGoogleProvider class
and rename your references accordingly.

note: Google services require an API key. Obtain here.
For more options and configurations, see the Google Maps developer docs.

const provider = new LegacyGoogleProvider({
  params: {
    key: '__YOUR_GOOGLE_KEY__',
  },
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Optional parameters

Google supports a number of optional parameters. As Google requires those parameters to be added to the url, they can be added to the params key of the provider.

All options defined next to the params key, would have been added to the request body.

const provider = new LegacyGoogleProvider({
  params: {
    key: '__YOUR_GOOGLE_KEY__',
    language: 'nl', // render results in Dutch
    region: 'nl', // prioritize matches within The Netherlands
  },
});

note: LocationIQ services require an API key. Obtain here.
For more options and configurations, see the LocationIQ developer docs.

const provider = new LocationIQProvider({
  params: {
    key: '__YOUR_LOCATIONIQ_KEY__',
  },
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

note: MapBox services require an API key. Obtain here.
For more options and configurations, see the MapBox developer docs on the Search API.

const provider = new MapBoxProvider({
  params: {
    access_token: '__YOUR_MAPBOX_KEY__',
  },
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

note: OpenCage services require an API key. Obtain here.
For more options and configurations, see the OpenCage developer docs.

const provider = new OpenCageProvider({
  params: {
    key: '__YOUR_OPENCAGE_KEY__',
  },
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

note: OpenGeocoding requires an API key. Create one in the dashboard. For use in a web page, create a browser key and restrict it to your site's origins. The key is sent in a header, never in the url.

Suggestions while typing come from the autocomplete endpoint and don't count as requests. They have no coordinates, so picking one looks it up by its id, and that counts as one request. Reverse geocoding counts as one request too. See the API docs.

const provider = new OpenGeocodingProvider({
  apiKey: '__YOUR_OPENGEOCODING_KEY__',
});

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Optional parameters

params go to the autocomplete endpoint: countryCode limits suggestions to one country, limit sets their number (up to 15), includeLandmarks adds notable landmarks, and focus.lat/focus.lon rank places near a position higher.

const provider = new OpenGeocodingProvider({
  apiKey: '__YOUR_OPENGEOCODING_KEY__',
  params: {
    countryCode: 'nl',
    limit: 5,
  },
});

Your own proxy

A browser key is visible to anyone who opens the page. To keep the key on your server, run a proxy that adds the x-api-key header and forwards /v1/autocomplete, /v1/retrieve and /v1/reverse to https://api.opengeocoding.com. Then point the provider to it with host:

const provider = new OpenGeocodingProvider({
  apiKey: '',
  host: 'https://example.com/geocode',
});

Self-hosting

The OpenGeocoding engine is source-available and can be run on your own servers under the terms of its license, PolyForm Shield 1.0.0. Read the license first: it doesn't allow offering a product that competes with OpenGeocoding. The data in the index has its own licenses too, such as ODbL for OpenStreetMap.

A self-hosted engine has a different API than the hosted one (Connect RPC instead of /v1/... routes) and no authentication, so host can't point to it directly. Put a proxy in front of it that offers the routes above, or write a custom provider for its API.

For more options and configurations, see the OpenStreetMap Nominatim wiki.

const provider = new OpenStreetMapProvider();

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Usage policy

The public Nominatim server (nominatim.openstreetmap.org) is free, but it has a usage policy. The short version:

your app must be identifiable. Browsers send a referer, which is enough. Where that is missing (e.g. in an app built with Electron), add the email parameter.

search-as-you-type is not allowed on the public server, and it doesn't work well anyway, because Nominatim can't search partial words. Turn off autoComplete on the control, or run your own Nominatim, or use another provider for autocomplete.

keep the number of requests low. The control can help with minQueryLength, autoCompleteDelay and cacheSize.

The email parameter is not a login and doesn't give you more requests. It lets the Nominatim admins tell your (small) traffic apart from scrapers they need to block.

const provider = new OpenStreetMapProvider({
  params: {
    email: 'john@example.com', // identifies your app to the Nominatim admins
  },
});

map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    autoComplete: false, // search-as-you-type is not allowed on nominatim.openstreetmap.org
  }),
);

Optional parameters

OpenStreetMap Nominatim supports a number of optional parameters. As the api requires those parameters to be added to the url, they can be added to the params key of the provider.

All options defined next to the params key, would have been added to the request body.

const provider = new OpenStreetMapProvider({
  params: {
    'accept-language': 'nl', // render results in Dutch
    countrycodes: 'nl', // limit search results to the Netherlands
    addressdetails: 1, // include additional address detail parts
    format: 'jsonv2', // the default is json
  },
});

Limiting the results to an area

Nominatim can leave out results outside an area. With countrycodes it only searches the given countries (ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 codes). With viewbox and bounded: 1 it only searches inside a box, given as west,north,east,south (longitude, latitude):

<Map provider="OpenStreetMap" providerParams={{ countrycodes: 'us,ca' }} />

// the United States and Canada
const countries = new OpenStreetMapProvider({
  params: { countrycodes: 'us,ca' },
});

// a box around the contiguous United States
const box = new OpenStreetMapProvider({
  params: { viewbox: '-125,49,-66,24', bounded: 1 },
});

Without bounded, the viewbox only puts the results inside the box first. Other providers have their own parameters for this, like bbox for Photon or boundary.rect for Pelias, see the page of each provider.

For more options and configurations, see the PDOK Locatieserver.

const provider = new PdokNlProvider();

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Optional parameters

PDOK Locatieserver supports a number of optional parameters. As the api requires those parameters to be added to the url, they can be added to the params key of the provider.

All options defined next to the params key, would have been added to the request body.

const provider = new PdokNlProvider({
  params: {
    fq: 'gemeentenaam:Leiden', // limit search results to municipality
    lat: 0, // Latitude in degree
    lon: 0, // Longitude in degree
  },
});

Pelias is an open-source geocoder powered completely by open data, available freely to everyone.

In order to use this provider you'll need to have your own Pelias server running.

The Docker repository provides the quickest path to running your own server.

See the Pelias documentation for more detailed information about the available endpoints and query parameters.

Looking for a hosted version of Pelias? check out the Geocode Earth provider.

// Pelias servers are self-hosted so you'll need to configure the 'options.host' string
// to identify where requests to your running pelias/api server instance should be sent.
// note: you SHOULD include the scheme, domain and port but NOT any path or parameters.
const provider = new PeliasProvider({ host: 'http://localhost:4000' });

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Optional parameters

Pelias supports a wide range of number of optional parameters which can be applied to every request using the params object:

const provider = new PeliasProvider({
  params: {
    size: 5, // limit the total number of results returned
    lang: 'nl', // render results in Dutch
    'boundary.country': 'NL', // limit search results to the Netherlands
    layers: 'address,street', // limmit which layers are queried
  },
});

Or individually on a per-request basis:

const results = await provider.search({
  query: {
    text: 'example',
    'focus.point.lat': 1.11, // score results nearer to the focus point higher
    'focus.point.lon': 2.22,
  },
});

Photon is an open-source geocoder for OpenStreetMap data, built for search as you type. It works without an API key.

The public server at photon.komoot.io is free for reasonable use, without guarantees. For heavy traffic, run your own server and point the provider to it with host.

const provider = new PhotonProvider();

// or your own server
const ownProvider = new PhotonProvider({ host: 'https://photon.example.com' });

// add to leaflet


map.addControl(
  new GeoSearchControl({
    provider,
    style: 'bar',
  }),
);

Optional parameters

Photon supports parameters like lang, limit, a location bias with lat/lon, or bbox. Pass them in params:

const provider = new PhotonProvider({
  params: {
    lang: 'de', // results in German
    limit: 5,
  },
});

5.0 fixes a lot of long-standing issues, and some of those fixes change what you see. The API is the same: nothing was removed or renamed, and the browser support didn't change. Each section shows what changed from 4.x (red) to 5.0 (green), and how to get the old behaviour back if you relied on it.

Dependencies and requirements

Package or requirement	4.x	5.0	What to do
leaflet	^1.6.0 (optional)	^1.6.0 (optional)	nothing
@googlemaps/js-api-loader	^1.16.6 (optional)	^2.1.3 (optional)	only if you use GoogleProvider and added it yourself, see below
@types/google.maps	not included	^3.53.1	nothing, you can drop it if you only added it for us
TypeScript		4.7 or newer	nothing for most projects
Browsers	ES2016 (no IE11)	ES2016 (no IE11)	nothing
Server-side rendering	not supported	leaflet-geosearch/providers	see Package

Updating the package

{/* prettier-ignore */}

{
  "dependencies": {
    "leaflet-geosearch": "^4.4.0" // [!code --]
    "leaflet-geosearch": "^5.0.0" // [!code ++]
  }
}

@googlemaps/js-api-loader 2

GoogleProvider loads the Maps JavaScript API with @googlemaps/js-api-loader. It's an optional dependency, so npm installs it with leaflet-geosearch. If your own package.json lists the 1.x loader too, update it, otherwise npm keeps a second copy for you:

{/* prettier-ignore */}

{
  "dependencies": {
    "@googlemaps/js-api-loader": "^1.16.6" // [!code --]
    "@googlemaps/js-api-loader": "^2.1.3" // [!code ++]
  }
}

The provider options don't change, see Google for the few that are gone.

@types/google.maps

The type declarations of GoogleProvider refer to the google.maps namespace. In 4.x, TypeScript projects without @types/google.maps failed to compile (#349). 5.0 installs it with the package. If you only added it for leaflet-geosearch, you can remove it:

{/* prettier-ignore */}

{
  "devDependencies": {
    "@types/google.maps": "^3.53.1" // [!code --]
  }
}

TypeScript

The types are checked with TypeScript 4.7 to 5.9, with moduleResolution set to node, bundler and node16. new GeoSearchControl(...) type-checks now, in 4.x only GeoSearchControl(...) did (#334).

Labels are escaped

The default resultFormat and popupFormat escape the label, since it comes from the provider (OpenStreetMap names are user edited).

{/* prettier-ignore */}

new GeoSearchControl({ provider });
// a label like "<b>Amsterdam</b>" is put into the page as html // [!code --]
// a label like "<b>Amsterdam</b>" shows up as text // [!code ++]

If your provider returns html in its labels on purpose, render it with your own formatters:

new GeoSearchControl({
  provider,
  resultFormat: ({ result }) => result.label,
  popupFormat: ({ result }) => result.label,
});

A picked suggestion isn't searched again

Picking a suggestion (click, or arrows and enter) shows it right away. Suggestions without coordinates are still searched.

{/* prettier-ignore */}

new GeoSearchControl({ provider });
// type "ams"        → provider.search({ query: 'ams' })
// pick "Amsterdam"  → provider.search({ query: 'Amsterdam' }) again, // [!code --]
//                     and the first result of that search is shown // [!code --]
// pick "Amsterdam"  → shown right away, no second request // [!code ++]

To always search again, like before:

new GeoSearchControl({ provider, requeryOnSelect: true });

autoClose keeps the marker

With autoClose and without keepResult, the marker of a picked result was removed right after it was added, so the map moved to a place without a marker. The marker and its popup stay now. The text in the box is still cleared without keepResult, and closing the box by hand or pressing escape still removes the marker.

{/* prettier-ignore */}

new GeoSearchControl({ provider, autoClose: true });
// pick "Amsterdam" → the map moves there, without a marker // [!code --]
// pick "Amsterdam" → the map moves there and the marker stays // [!code ++]

For no marker, like before:

new GeoSearchControl({ provider, autoClose: true, showMarker: false });

Failed searches fire an event

A failed search (offline, a server error, a blocked request) fires geosearch/error on the map, and the control shows its error style until the user types again.

{/* prettier-ignore */}

// a failed request ended in an unhandled promise rejection // [!code --]
window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (event) => { // [!code --]
  console.warn('search failed', event.reason); // [!code --]
}); // [!code --]
map.on('geosearch/error', ({ error, query, source, provider }) => { // [!code ++]
  console.warn(`search for "${query}" failed`, error); // [!code ++]
}); // [!code ++]

await control.onSubmit({ query: 'amsterdam' }); // rejected // [!code --]
await control.onSubmit({ query: 'amsterdam' }); // resolves, the event has the error // [!code ++]

Fewer autocomplete requests

Autocomplete no longer searches again for the same text (keys like shift or tab), drops answers that come back after a newer search started, and also searches after a paste or on mobile keyboards. Nothing to change in your code. Custom providers get an optional signal to cancel outdated requests, ignoring it is fine:

{/* prettier-ignore */}

class MyProvider {
  async search({ query }) { // [!code --]
    const response = await fetch(`https://example.com/search?q=${query}`); // [!code --]
  async search({ query, signal }) { // [!code ++]
    const response = await fetch(`https://example.com/search?q=${query}`, { signal }); // [!code ++]
    return this.parse(await response.json());
  }
}

Options

Options passed as undefined keep their default, and marker options are merged with the defaults. Wrapper components often pass every prop, set or not.

{/* prettier-ignore */}

new GeoSearchControl({ provider, marker: undefined });
// every search throws "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'draggable')" // [!code --]
// the default marker // [!code ++]

new GeoSearchControl({ provider, maxSuggestions: undefined });
// every suggestion is shown // [!code --]
// 5 suggestions, the default // [!code ++]

new GeoSearchControl({ provider, marker: { draggable: true } });
// a draggable marker that keeps the default icon

Providers

Google

GoogleProvider uses @googlemaps/js-api-loader 2. The option names stay the same (apiKey, version, language, ...). If the page already loaded the Maps JavaScript API, the provider uses it instead of loading it again. url, nonce, retries and id don't exist in the new loader, they are ignored with a warning in the console:

{/* prettier-ignore */}

new GoogleProvider({
  apiKey: 'KEY',
  url: 'https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/js', // [!code --]
  nonce: 'abc', // [!code --]
  retries: 3, // [!code --]
});

Esri

{/* prettier-ignore */}

new EsriProvider(); // one suggestion // [!code --]
new EsriProvider(); // up to 5 suggestions // [!code ++]

One, like before:

new EsriProvider({ params: { maxLocations: 1 } });

OpenStreetMap, LocationIQ and CivilDefenseMap

{/* prettier-ignore */}

new OpenStreetMapProvider({ params: { format: 'jsonv2' } });
// still sends format=json // [!code --]
// sends format=jsonv2, json stays the default // [!code ++]

Package

the npm package no longer ships the tests, fixtures and the old docs theme.

package.json has "sideEffects": ["*.css"], so bundlers drop what you don't use. Importing the css still works.

@types/google.maps is installed with the package, TypeScript projects don't need to add it.

exports lists the types condition first. With moduleResolution: "bundler" or "node16", TypeScript found the types only through a fallback before.

the bundles keep their names and paths: dist/geosearch.js, geosearch.module.js, geosearch.umd.js, bundle.min.js and geosearch.css. Direct links like unpkg.com/leaflet-geosearch/dist/geosearch.umd.js keep working.

new entry leaflet-geosearch/providers with the providers only, without Leaflet. Importing the main entry loads Leaflet, which fails on the server ("window is not defined"). On the server the providers need a global fetch, which Node.js has since version 18:

{/* prettier-ignore */}

// Next.js, Nuxt, ...
 // [!code --]
 // [!code ++]

New in 5.0

These are new options, nothing changes unless you use them:

multiple providers in one control, with a provider select and a fallback: Multiple providers

zoomToBounds, fitBoundsOptions and flyTo: Zoom on a result

showResultListOnSubmit, minQueryLength and cacheSize: Options

reverse geocoding with provider.reverse(), and reverseOnDragEnd for a dragged marker: Reverse geocoding, Address of a dragged marker

reset(), setQuery() and setLabels(): Methods

resultListPosition, theme, searchIcon, markerPopup and popupOptions: Leaflet control

the geosearch/beforeshowlocation event: Events

the result list follows the ARIA combobox pattern for screen readers: Accessibility

the Photon provider: Photon

There are two ways in which leaflet-geosearch can be used. Direct usage, for example for address forms, or embedded in a leaflet map to search for points of interest.

Using the providers directly

All providers can be used without leaflet. You might want to bind them to a form;

const provider = new OpenStreetMapProvider();
const results = await provider.search({ query: input.value });

Reverse geocoding

reverse returns the places at a position, closest first, in the same format as search:

const provider = new OpenStreetMapProvider();
const [place] = await provider.reverse({ lat: 52.3731, lng: 4.8926 });

console.log(
  JSON.stringify({ label: place.label, x: place.x, y: place.y }, null, 2),
);

{
  "label": "Dam, Centrum, Amsterdam, Noord-Holland, Nederland, 1012 NP, Nederland",
  "x": 4.8926932,
  "y": 52.3733144
}

OpenStreetMap, LocationIQ, CivilDefenseMap, Photon, Pelias, Geocode Earth, Geoapify, GeoApiFr and PdokNl support it. The other providers reject with "This provider does not support reverse geocoding".

leaflet-geosearch imports Leaflet, which only runs in a browser. For server-side rendering (Next.js, Nuxt, ...), import the providers from leaflet-geosearch/providers. It has the same providers and doesn't import Leaflet:

Using the leaflet control

Or add the GeoSearchControl to the leaflet map instance, to render a search control on your map;

See Leaflet Control for more info about the the options this control provides.

const searchControl = new GeoSearchControl({
  provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider(),
  style: 'bar',
});

map.addControl(searchControl);

Leaflet 2

The control works with Leaflet 1.6 and up, and with Leaflet 2. Leaflet 2 has no factory functions like L.map() and no global L in its ES modules, so create the map with new Map() and import what you need by name. The control is used the same way:

const map = new Map('map').setView([52.09, 5.12], 8);
new TileLayer('https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png').addTo(map);

map.addControl(new GeoSearchControl({ provider: new OpenStreetMapProvider() }));

Without a bundler, load Leaflet 2's leaflet-global.js, which sets window.L, before the UMD build of leaflet-geosearch:

<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@2.0.0-alpha.1/dist/leaflet.css"
/>
<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet-geosearch@5/dist/geosearch.css"
/>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@2.0.0-alpha.1/dist/leaflet-global.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet-geosearch@5/dist/geosearch.umd.js"></script>

Leaflet 2 is only published as ES modules. The CommonJS build of leaflet-geosearch loads it with require('leaflet'), which works in Node 20.19+ and 22.12+.

Results

The search event of all providers return an array of result objects. The base structure is uniform between the providers. It contains objects matching the following interface:

interface result {
  x: number; // lon
  y: number; // lat
  label: string; // formatted address
  bounds: [
    [number, number], // south, west - lat, lon
    [number, number], // north, east - lat, lon
  ];
  raw: any; // raw provider result
}
